her pattern. For example, if they have a pair of W1s, which are terminal tiles. If they have a pung of 6s, which are non-terminal. So the pair is a terminal, contributing to Outside Hand (1 point). So total would be 4 (kong) + 2 (Mixed Double Pung) + 1 (Outside Hand) = 7 points. Still need one more.\n\nAlternatively, if the user has a pair of W1s and a pung of 6s, and the kong of J3. The pair is a terminal, so Outside Hand (1 point). Plus the kong (4) and Mixed Double Pung (2). Total 7. Still need one more.\n\nMaybe the user can get a Closed Wait (1 point) if they have only one way to win. But I'm not sure. The user is trying to form a winning hand, but the current hand is not winning yet. They need to discard a tile to allow for a winning hand.\n\nWait, the user is in the middle of the game. They have to discard a tile to optimize their chances of forming a winning hand. The key is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential meld and that allows them to form the necessary melds when they draw or discard.\n\nGiven the user's current hand, the best bet is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential meld. Looking at the tiles in hand: W1 (2), W2, W4, W6, T6, B6, B8.\n\nThe B8 is a single tile and not part of any meld. Discarding B8 might be the safest option. If the user discards B8, they can focus on forming the pung of 6s and the pair of W1s. Even though they still need another meld, maybe they can form it by drawing a tile.\n\nAlternatively, discarding W2 or W4 might help, but they are part of potential chows if they had the right tiles. Since they don't, it's better to discard B8.\n\nAnother angle: the user has a kong of J3 and a chow of W5-6-7. If they can form a pung of 6s and another pung, that would give them two pungs (Mixed Double Pung, 2 points) plus the kong (4 points). But they need another pung. The only possibility is if they can form another pung, but they don't have enough tiles.\n\nSo the best move is to discard B8, as it's not part of any potential meld and allows the user to keep the other tiles for forming the necessary melds.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: The user's hand has a kong of J3 and a chow of W5-6-7. To form a winning hand, they need two more melds and a pair. The best approach is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential meld. B8 is a single tile with no immediate use, making it the safest discard.\n\nReason: Discarding B8 preserves the potential to form a pung of 6s (T6, B6, W6) and a pair of W1s, which are critical for scoring. Keeping the W1s as a pair and forming the pung of 6s provides two melds and a pair, plus the existing kong and chow, potentially meeting the 8-point requirement. Other tiles like W2 or W4 are part of incomplete chows and less safe to discard.\n\nAnswer: Play B8"}
